course: dictionary
lesson: 1069
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have several meanings, and bound is one of them. To choose the right meaning, read the words around it, which are its context. «A red kangaroo can cover about eight metres in one bound» is about a jump, because cover eight metres in one bound tells you how far it goes in one leap.
explain: The same word can mean something very different in another sentence. «Liking football or rough games does not make a girl bound to fight» uses bound to in the sense of certain or likely to. Here bound to means that something is almost sure to happen, not that anyone is tied up.
explain: Ask yourself what the word is doing in the sentence. Is it a jump, a rule, a place or a feeling of certainty? Then swap your meaning into the sentence to check it still makes sense. If it does not, try another meaning from the list.
fact: Bound can be an adjective, a verb or a noun, so its job in the sentence helps you work out its meaning.
fact: «Bound to» means certain or very likely to happen, as in «bound to get caught».
fact: To bound means to jump or run with big springy leaps, as in «a puppy bounds at her».
fact: Bounds are the edges or limits of an area, and «out of bounds» means a place you are not allowed to enter.
fact: Bound can also mean tied up with rope so it cannot move, or going towards a place, as in «a train bound for Leeds».
exercise: «A red kangaroo can cover about eight metres in one bound.» What is a bound in this sentence? | A train going to Leeds | A rope tied round a parcel | The edge of a field | *One big jump
exercise: «There a puppy, enormous to tiny Alice, bounds at her.» What does bounds mean here? | Says hello politely | Sleeps quietly in the corner | *Jumps or runs at her with springy leaps | Is tied up with rope
exercise: «Liking football or rough games does not make a girl bound to fight.» What does bound to mean? | Going towards a place | Obliged by a rule | Tied up with rope | *Certain or likely to
test: Safety sign: «The roof is out of bounds.» What does out of bounds mean? | Far away from home | A place with lots of bounce | *A place pupils must not enter | The highest point of the school
test: «Gravity there is only one sixth of ours, so a child could bound like a kangaroo.» What does bound mean here? | *Jump with big springy leaps | Stay still and wait | Tie up tightly | Read quietly
exam: Which phrase means a place you are not allowed to go? | duty bound | homeward bound | *out of bounds | by leaps and bounds
